Long ago, in a remote part of Siberia, a small village community lived peacefully, sustaining themselves by cultivating the land and hunting. However, hard times came, food began to run out, and a war affecting everyone broke out between the villages. The inhabitants of the peaceful village had to quickly pack their belongings and embark on a hurried escape as enemy troops approached.

While camping in a safe place, their provisions dwindled alarmingly. The village chief ordered food to be rationed and distributed equally to everyone. He swore to severely punish anyone who dared to steal from the common supplies. The culprit would be flogged in front of everyone, no matter who the thief was.

A few days later, good and bad news was brought to the chief: the thief had been caught. Then the soldier fell silent and whispered sorrowfully to the chief that the thief was the chief's own old and frail mother. The chief was deeply shocked. How could he allow his mother to be flogged? On the other hand, if he pardoned his mother, his authority would crumble completely, and no one would respect him anymore. The chief knew his elderly mother wouldn't survive the punishment, but he concluded there was no other option but to stick to his word.

On the day of the punishment, the chief stood pale-faced as his mother was dragged to the village square. As the soldier performing the flogging raised his whip for the first strike, the chief could no longer bear it. He shouted, "Stop!", ordered his mother to be freed, took off his uniform and shirt, and stepped into his mother's place. He took the punishment meant for the guilty party upon himself and suffered the flogging on behalf of another.

This story is a depiction of true love. Love that is available to everyone. It is called grace and mercy.